WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.879
So now I want to show you another

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:05.279
type of turtle program we can send to

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:07.600
the to the plotter machine.
- (You're)

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.320
(there, waiting for your next job)
- Yeah,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.679
and we're going to continue with this

00:00:11.679 --> 00:00:13.440
idea of just giving it a list of angles

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.599
to turn through. And I just need to find

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:17.199
some source of

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:20.640
angles, and I thought maybe let's look at

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.600
the digits of numbers like pi and that

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.240
kind of thing. Here we have our original

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.160
thing where we started exploring the

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.960
Euler spiral; like what if I just start

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.070
sending the digits of pi?

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.830
...8,4,6.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.600
(Wow you know that many off by heart?)
- I know

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.640
more than that but-

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.800
The next one's a 2. You always

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.719
need to know one more.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:51.199
If I sent this this would just be like

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:53.920
3 radians, 1 radian and stuff it- I

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.440
think what would make more sense would

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.440
be to do- well what I've marked up on on

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.480
this here,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.840
I've split this into ten directions.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.559
So

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:05.439
if I do- for the first three if I do one

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:07.600
two three, I could- that might make more

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.400
sense. So I'll be turning three tenths of

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.680
a full turn,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.119
and then I'll turn

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:15.200
you know then I'll turn one tenth, then

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.840
four tenths, one tenth, five, nine;
and then

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.360
we'll see what- see what picture gets

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.560
drawn. 
- (So does that mean 1 will be)

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.840
(36 degrees?)
- Yeah, yeah we're doing

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.880
multiples of 36 degrees. So yeah let's

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.680
put in that, 36 degrees.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.200
It would have been pointing this way and

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.200
it turned back three tenths to go up

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.180
this way.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.840
And then it here it turned just 36

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.910
degrees, the smallest amount it can turn,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.320
and so on.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:46.240
Here when it got to 5 you'll notice that

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.280
it just did a

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.040
180 degree turn

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.399
because 5

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:53.040
tenths is a half. Any time you see a 5

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.640
that means it goes back;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.399
so if I see two fives basically it

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:58.960
doesn't do anything right?

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.240
Yeah so

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.079
let's draw more. We're going to stick to

00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:04.479
base 10 to start with, and and then it

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.280
will do this picture, it'll plot the

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.360
turtle drawing for that number.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.360
So here we have pi

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.430
in base 10, and let's do

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.040
3,000 steps of pi. 
I got this picture here,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.920
kind of like a fractal island or a

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.840
sort of wild walk

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.840
going around everywhere. Let's do more

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.360
digits, see what it looks like, so here's

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.506
10,000.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.120
Our original picture, I think was up in

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.680
this corner here, now it's branched off

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.630
and gone over here.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.010
20,000

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.200
100,000

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.239
So like here is some

00:03:10.239 --> 00:03:12.700
way of visualising the digits of pi;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.640
let's just sort of come out from playing

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.030
around with turtle programs.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.400
(I've got so many questions coming to my)

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.840
(mind like)

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.920
(where will it ultimately go? Will it ever)

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.840
(come back to the start? Will every)

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.000
(possible shape be formed at some point?)

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.599
(Like, you know you know how they)

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:45.120
(say the monkeys writing Shakespeare?)

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.040
(Will you eventually draw the Mona Lisa)

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.120
(just by luck?)
- Right, yeah yeah. I have

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.879
those same questions as well. One

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:52.640
conjecture about pi is that it's normal,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.319
the digits of pi are normal, meaning that

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:56.560
every possible sequence that you could

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:57.840
think of:

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.080
0,1,2,3 up to 9 would be

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.680
in the digits of pi.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.680
That- I guess that means if you had some

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.519
shape that you could draw with 36

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:08.000
degrees turn you would expect that

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.920
shape to eventually show up in this

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.280
drawing.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.120
I guess you don't know exactly where in

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.799
the paper it would be

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:17.199
right? So I'm- so I

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:20.400
I don't know if you would expect after

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.919
drawing for infinitely long, would you

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:23.360
just have like a black sheet of paper

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.280
that's fully filled in with ink?

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.400
(When you get a very long string of)

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.479
(0s you'll get like really long)

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:32.560
(straight lines?)
- Long straight line yeah.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.479
Another question I think is natural to

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:36.560
ask is like, if I gave you this picture

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.080
could you have worked out the digits of

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.600
pi?

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.320
Right? Or if I sent someone this as like

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.320
a secret message or I broadcast this

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.160
across the universe as like a sign that

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.160
there's some intelligence on Earth; would

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.759
it be a good way to show like, hey we

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:52.720
know about pi? Let's do 3,000 so

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.560
we get this kind of like

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.040
dancing off away from the main kind of

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:57.900
hub.

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:42.240
This is pi base 10,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.440
almost 3,000 digits. Base 10 is arbitrary

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.360
right? So I mean you want to try a

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.310
different base? Got one in mind?

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.800
(Well let's go base 3.) 
- Base 3 is cool.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.720
So let's let's change the base to 3

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.720
and let's boost up to 10,000

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.120
points. So one cool thing about base

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.199
3 is that our our turns are going to

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:03.520
be uh 120 degrees,

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:04.479
that means that we're going to be

00:06:04.479 --> 00:06:06.960
constrained to this triangular grid,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.880
triangles that tessellate the plane.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.960
Whereas with base 10

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.120
we have this weird 36 degree angle, it's

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.199
kind of like pentagons right? And

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:17.280
pentagons don't tile the plane so yeah

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.479
you can

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:20.479
get a real mess. You're always going to

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:22.720
have sort of collections of triangles if

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.720
you use base 3. 
- (So will base 4)

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.070
(give us squares?)

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.400
Yeah that's right!

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.400
So now we've got squares. I like this as

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.600
maybe

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.280
if I needed to generate some kind of

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:36.240
like roads for a zoomed out city in a

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.479
video game or something, you might

00:06:38.479 --> 00:06:40.960
you know create a network like this.
- (They)

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.440
(look like world maps to me)
- Yeah

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.560
some people say they're kind of like-

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.280
like ways to generate an island.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.599
It looks like Japan maybe. We're could do

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:50.800
more uh-

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.930
let's do 100,000 in base 4.

00:07:12.479 --> 00:07:14.639
So I'm- I chose pi because I want like

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:17.039
an infinite list of turns that is just

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:18.639
going to spew out the Mona Lisa at some

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:19.840
point.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.440
A less interesting digit sequence would

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.680
just be a rational number. So let's change

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:24.960
pi to

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.960
1/7th. I like 1/7th because it's

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.800
0.142857

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.880
142857... If you do

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.319
that enough you're just gonna end up

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:33.599
back where you started in the same

00:07:33.599 --> 00:07:35.520
orientation and you're gonna create a

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.669
rotationally symmetric picture like this.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.599
Could make it like one over some bigger

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:48.639
prime;

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:50.879
like I guess 119 is a prime

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:53.840
probably? Then you get a like a a more

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.360
detailed but still rotationally

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.000
symmetric picture. It's kind of

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.000
a nice way to see a stark difference

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.840
between rational numbers and irrational

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.030
numbers.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.720
Do more irrational numbers, will we try e?

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.160
So let's do e,

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.440
base 10,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.529
100,000.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.840
There you go, that's e.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:33.519
(What's going on here? That's a cool)

00:08:33.519 --> 00:08:34.800
(little place,)

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.320
(there's a lagoon)
- Yeah this seems

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.080
like an unlikely configuration.
- (That)

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:40.320
(looks like Britain, up there)
- Yeah yeah

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.719
Oh yeah! We're here right now.
- (Yep)

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:44.360
(Scotland, yeah)

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:45.920
I like

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.680
golden ratio?

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.360
So what? You got square root of 5 plus

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.000
1. In some sense the most irrational

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.180
number.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.440
I don't know if it gives a

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.519
sort of more squiggly map but it looks

00:08:57.519 --> 00:08:58.780
cool to me.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.200
(Imagine if you just put some arbitrary)

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.360
(number in one day, like randomly, and)

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:28.560
(pressed it and your face appeared!)
- Yeah!

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:29.680
Or

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.200
conversely, could somebody send me the

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.130
number that draws my face?

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.760
This is the Feynman point, so six 9s,

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.160
99999. So that

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.040
won't be beaten- there is a string of

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.640
seven 3s

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.720
but that takes another seven hundred and

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:50.240
ten thousand digits, so we'll be nearly

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.920
three quarters of the way down the mile of

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.560
pi before we find a longer string of the

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.560
same digit and the first one is within

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.519
the first two metres.

